.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.env
Based on your GitHub repository view, you should immediately remove .env (in both root and server/) and server/.env.production from Git. Because they are visible in your public/remote repository on GitHub, any API keys, database passwords, or JWT secrets inside them are currently exposed.
## Files Currently Committed That Should NOT Be

* server/.env & root .env: Never commit real environment files to GitHub. Instead, rename them to .env.example (or .env.template) with dummy placeholder values so teammates know which variables to set locally.
* server/.env.production: Production credentials and secrets are especially dangerous to keep in version control. Remove this file from Git and inject production environment variables directly through your hosting provider or Docker Compose deployment environment.

## Files & Folders That ARE Safe to Keep Committed
Everything else visible in your server/ directory belongs in your repository:

* Configuration & Dependency Files: .dockerignore, .gitignore, .pre-commit-config.yaml, .python-version, Dockerfile, alembic.ini, pyproject.toml, uv.lock, and README.md should all stay tracked.
* Application Code & Migrations: alembic/, api/, app/, dependencies/, repositories/, services/, src/server/, and tests/ are your core codebase and belong in Git.

## How to Fix the Exposed .env Files

   1. Rotate your secrets immediately: If this repository is public (or shared widely), assume any passwords, tokens, or API keys inside .env and .env.production are compromised and generate new ones.
   2. Untrack the files without deleting them locally: Run these commands from your project root:
   
   git rm --cached .env server/.env server/.env.production
   
   3. Verify .gitignore rules: Make sure your root .gitignore includes .env and .env.* (with an exception like !.env.example if you create template files).
   4. Commit and push the removal: Run git commit -m "chore: untrack env files" and git push.

If those .env files contain sensitive production secrets that need to be completely wiped from your past Git commit history (not just the latest commit), let me know and I can walk you through scrubbing them with git filter-repo!

